// Contract content (GAMEPLAY.md §6/§9) — data, never hardcoded in sim_core.
// This is the `contracts.ron` CLAUDE.md's Project Structure section and
// GAMEPLAY.md §9 have both been anticipating ("Contracts need their own data
// file ... following the same schema_version convention as the other data
// files"); it replaces the single `Contract::hardcoded()` milestone 5's
// Economy MVP shipped with, and — more importantly — it is what makes
// SEVERAL contracts able to run at once.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader (`sim_core::sim::contracts::ContractRegistry`) rejects a
// mismatched version rather than silently misreading a changed shape, the
// same way `recipes.ron`'s and `machines.ron`'s loaders do.
//
// WHAT A CONTRACT IS (GAMEPLAY.md §6). A standing agreement to supply a
// `mix` of items every `cadence_ticks` ticks, for `payout` money each time
// the mix is delivered in full. It is deliberately NOT a spot market: the
// mix is all-or-nothing, so half a delivery earns nothing, and that is the
// whole reason the game rewards a factory that can hold several different
// lines running at once rather than one maximally-profitable one.
//
// FILE ORDER IS PRIORITY. Every contract draws from ONE shared pool of
// collected-but-undelivered stock (see `sim_core/src/sim/economy.rs`'s
// module doc for the full allocation rule and why it is the way it is). When
// two contracts fall due on the same tick and there isn't enough stock for
// both, the one listed FIRST here is filled first. That is a deterministic
// tiebreak (ARCHITECTURE.md §3.9), not a coincidence of iteration order —
// reordering this list genuinely changes who gets paid under scarcity.
//
// EVERY CONTRACT BELOW ASKS FOR `boxed_ketchup`, AND THAT IS NOT A DESIGN
// STATEMENT. GAMEPLAY.md §6's mixes are meant to name several *different*
// sauces; the chain in `data/recipes.ron` currently produces exactly one
// sellable product, and the delivery dock at the chain's tail only ever
// receives what that one line's final stage makes, so any contract naming a
// different item would be permanently unsatisfiable content rather than
// interesting content. The multi-item `mix` shape is fully implemented and tested —
// what is missing is a second sauce line to point it at, which is future
// content work (GAMEPLAY.md §2's tier table), not a limitation of this file.
// What the board below DOES create today is the real thing: independent
// cadences competing for a factory's throughput, on a scale that keeps
// asking for one more line.
//
// BALANCE NOTE (rewritten 2026-09-11, when the board grew from three
// contracts to eight). The shipped chain's bottleneck is the 240-tick
// cooking vat, so ONE line's steady-state throughput is roughly one
// `boxed_ketchup` per ~245 ticks, i.e. about 4.0 per 1000 ticks. The
// board is sized in that unit — "lines' worth of demand":
//
//   starter tier   (corner_shop + diner_supply + market_stall)  ~3.9 / 1000
//   growth tier    (the five orders below them)                ~17.7 / 1000
//   whole board                                                ~21.5 / 1000
//
// The starter tier is deliberately just inside ONE line's capacity: a
// first factory kept fed (hoppers and tank on auto-fill) can serve all
// three, and one left to run dry starts missing windows. Everything past
// it is what the second, third, fourth and fifth line are FOR. Before the
// growth tier existed, the board absorbed one line's output and not a box
// more, so the money a first line earned bought a second line that had
// nothing to sell to — the game stalled at exactly the point where a
// factory-builder should be taking off. Now the whole board takes a little
// over five lines, so there is always an order that the next line can go
// after, and a fully built-out board is a real target rather than a ceiling
// reached in the first ten minutes.
//
// Rates per box RISE WITH COMMITMENT, on every tier: the small, frequent
// order pays worst per box (24) and the big, slow pallet order pays best
// (40). Big orders are also the ones that are impossible to serve from a
// single line — a 20-box pallet on a 5000-tick cadence needs a whole line
// devoted to it — so a better per-box rate is the reward for building the
// capacity to reach it, and a player who cannot yet fill it loses nothing
// but that window's payout (see PENALTY TERMS below).
//
// PRIORITY ORDER IS SMALLEST FIRST, on purpose. When several orders fall
// due on the same tick (every 30 000 ticks every cadence on the board lines
// up), the starter orders are filled before the growth tier gets a look
// in — so a first factory's income is never eaten by a pallet order the
// player has not built for, and a big order that CAN be filled still is,
// out of whatever the small ones left. Within the growth tier the order
// again runs small to large.
//
// A box that no contract has ordered is still ingredients turned into
// unsold stock (`data/ingredients.ron`, ~11 per box), so a line built
// beyond the board's demand is still a loss — the board grew, the rule did
// not.
//
// PENALTY TERMS ARE NOT HERE YET, ON PURPOSE. GAMEPLAY.md §9 lists them as
// part of this file's eventual shape, but GAMEPLAY.md §11 still has "what
// exactly happens when a delivery interval is missed" as an open question
// (immediate loss / grace period / reputation meter). A missed window today
// costs that window's payout and nothing else. Adding a `penalty` field
// nothing reads would be inventing an answer to that question in data;
// it lands here — with a `schema_version` bump — once the question is
// actually decided.
(
    schema_version: 1,
    contracts: [
        // The starter order: exactly milestone 5's hardcoded contract
        // (2 boxes every 1000 ticks for 50), kept numerically identical so
        // that the first thing a new player — and the full-chain integration
        // test — sees is unchanged by this file's arrival.
        Contract(
            id: "corner_shop",
            name: "Corner Shop Standing Order",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 2)],
            cadence_ticks: 1000,
            payout: 50,
        ),
        // The reason to keep the line running rather than babysitting one
        // window: a bigger box count on a much slower cadence, at the best
        // per-unit rate on the board (30/box).
        Contract(
            id: "diner_supply",
            name: "Roadside Diner Group",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 3)],
            cadence_ticks: 2500,
            payout: 90,
        ),
        // The small, awkward one: a single box on a cadence that lines up
        // with neither of the others, at the worst per-unit rate (24/box).
        // Its job is to be the order that gets squeezed out when the line
        // stalls — which is exactly the pressure a single contract could
        // never create.
        Contract(
            id: "market_stall",
            name: "Saturday Market Stall",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 1)],
            cadence_ticks: 1500,
            payout: 24,
        ),

        // --- The growth tier (added 2026-09-11) ---
        // Everything from here down is more than one line can serve, by
        // design: it is the demand a second, third and fourth line are built
        // to meet. Small to large, per the priority rule in the header.

        // A steady institutional order: four boxes on the market stall's
        // cadence, at a rate a shade above the diner's (32/box). The first
        // order a second line makes reachable — a line and a half serves
        // the starter tier and this.
        Contract(
            id: "school_canteens",
            name: "District School Canteens",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 4)],
            cadence_ticks: 1500,
            payout: 128,
        ),
        // Six boxes every 2000 ticks (34/box) — three quarters of a line
        // on its own.
        Contract(
            id: "wholesale_grocer",
            name: "Wholesale Grocer Depot",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 6)],
            cadence_ticks: 2000,
            payout: 204,
        ),
        // A whole line's worth on a 2000-tick cadence (36/box): the order
        // that justifies a line of its own.
        Contract(
            id: "burger_franchise",
            name: "Burger Franchise Commissary",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 8)],
            cadence_ticks: 2000,
            payout: 288,
        ),
        // Also a whole line's worth, but in bigger, slower batches (38/box):
        // twelve boxes must be standing on the dock at once, so the shared
        // pool has to be allowed to build up rather than skimmed by the
        // starter orders — which is what listing it AFTER them costs the
        // player, and what the higher rate pays for.
        Contract(
            id: "stadium_concessions",
            name: "Stadium Concessions",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 12)],
            cadence_ticks: 3000,
            payout: 456,
        ),
        // The pallet order: twenty boxes every 5000 ticks at the best rate
        // on the board (40/box). A whole line's throughput, held back for
        // 5000 ticks — the endgame order on this board until a second sauce
        // exists to write a mixed order for.
        Contract(
            id: "export_pallet",
            name: "Export Pallet Order",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 20)],
            cadence_ticks: 5000,
            payout: 800,
        ),
    ],
)
